        <!-- Trade-offs -->
        <mxCell id="180" value="5 · Key Trade-offs (each vs a NAMED alternative)" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2170" width="600" height="26" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="181" value="&lt;b&gt;Continuous batching&lt;/b&gt; vs static batching vs no batching&#10;Static batching wastes the GPU: the whole batch waits for the longest generation. No batching wastes it worse —&#10;a single decode step underutilises the device by ~50x. Continuous batching admits and evicts per token step.&#10;&lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; the scheduler is now on the critical path of every token, and per-request latency depends on&#10;who else is in the batch — a noisy-neighbour effect you must bound with a max batch size.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;SSE&lt;/b&gt; vs WebSocket vs long-poll — see API section. Chosen for HTTP/2 + native browser reconnect.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Stateful workers + affinity routing&lt;/b&gt; vs stateless round-robin&#10;Round-robin is impossible mid-generation: the KV cache lives on one GPU. Affinity also buys prefix-cache hits.&#10;&lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; load balancing gets hard, a node loss kills every in-flight stream on it, and drains take&#10;up to a full generation (~25s) to complete.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Warm pool&lt;/b&gt; vs pure on-demand scale-out&#10;Provisioning (minutes) + weight load (1–5 min) is far longer than any acceptable TTFT, so autoscaling cannot&#10;react to a spike — it must anticipate it. &lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; you pay for idle GPUs. Quantify it: at ~7,500 peak&#10;GPUs, a 15% warm buffer is ~1,100 idle GPUs — a real line item, justified against a shed-traffic alternative.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;Priority queue with shedding&lt;/b&gt; vs fair-share vs first-come-first-served&#10;FCFS lets free traffic starve paid SLOs. Fair-share protects nobody's p99. Strict priority + free-tier&#10;shedding meets the paid SLO. &lt;b&gt;Given up:&lt;/b&gt; free-tier starvation under sustained load — bound it with a&#10;reserved floor of capacity so free never goes to literally zero." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#E8F5EC;strokeColor=#1A7F37;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="2201" width="820" height="560" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

        <!-- Deep dives -->
        <mxCell id="190" value="6 · Deep Dives" style="text;html=1;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=4;fontSize=14;fontStyle=1;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="910" y="2170" width="400" height="26" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="191" value="&lt;b&gt;A. The core hard problem — GPU memory is the real scheduler&lt;/b&gt;&#10;Naive: route to the least-loaded worker by request count.&#10;&lt;b&gt;Break:&lt;/b&gt; requests are not equal. A 100k-context request's KV cache can be&#10;100x a short one's. Counting requests overcommits GPU RAM → OOM mid-&#10;generation → every stream on that worker dies at once.&#10;&lt;b&gt;Fix:&lt;/b&gt; admission control on &lt;i&gt;bytes&lt;/i&gt;: kvBytesPerToken x (promptLen + maxTokens)&#10;must fit in freeKvBytes before dispatch. Paged KV blocks so memory is allocated&#10;incrementally and fragmentation doesn't strand capacity. Preemption policy:&#10;evict/requeue the newest low-priority request rather than OOM the box.&#10;&lt;b&gt;Trade-off:&lt;/b&gt; reserving for maxTokens over-reserves for requests that stop early;&#10;incremental paging recovers that but risks a mid-flight allocation failure.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;B. The scale break — 10x traffic, or one viral moment&lt;/b&gt;&#10;At 3e4 msg/s peak the fleet is ~7,500 GPUs. Push 10x and there is no fleet to&#10;buy: GPU supply is a hard external ceiling, unlike every other system where you&#10;add nodes. So the design must degrade instead of scale:&#10;  • shed free tier first (503 + Retry-After), then downgrade free traffic to the&#10;    small model, then cap maxTokens per request&#10;  • queue paid traffic with a deadline; surface honest queue position&#10;  • multi-region overflow routing to wherever capacity exists&#10;&lt;b&gt;This is the senior conversation: the fix is admission policy, not capacity.&lt;/b&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;C. Hot keys / hot partitions&lt;/b&gt;&#10;• Long-context requests are the hot key — they monopolise KV memory. Isolate&#10;  them onto a dedicated long-context pool so they can't starve short chats.&#10;• A single shared system prompt is a hot &lt;i&gt;prefix&lt;/i&gt; — that's a gift, not a problem:&#10;  cache its KV once per worker and skip prefill for it entirely.&#10;• Model skew: 90% small / 10% large means the fleet split must be dynamic, or&#10;  the large-model pool sits idle while small-model requests queue.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;D. Failure of an in-flight stream&lt;/b&gt;&#10;Worker dies at token 300 of 500. The KV cache is gone and cannot be recovered.&#10;Options: (i) restart the generation from scratch on another worker — correct but&#10;the user sees a stutter and you pay twice; (ii) fail the request. Choose (i) for&#10;paid, (ii) for free. The client resumes via fromSeq and the gateway replays its&#10;buffered tail, so the user sees a pause, not a broken answer. Non-determinism&#10;means a restart may produce different text — pin the seed per requestId.&#10;&#10;&lt;b&gt;E. Operability — how do you know it's wrong?&lt;/b&gt;&#10;• Alert on &lt;b&gt;queue depth and TTFT p99 per tier&lt;/b&gt;, never on GPU utilisation —&#10;  a healthy batched GPU is pinned at 100% by design, so utilisation says nothing.&#10;• Track TPOT p99: a rising inter-token latency means batches are too large.&#10;• Track KV-memory headroom per worker and preemption rate.&#10;• Track $/M tokens as a first-class SLO — at this fleet size cost IS a health metric.&#10;• Watch weight-load time and warm-pool hit rate; a cold-start storm is invisible&#10;  in request metrics until TTFT has already blown through the SLO." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=10;spacingTop=8;fillColor=#E8F5EC;strokeColor=#1A7F37;fontSize=11;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="910" y="2201" width="810" height="900" as="geometry"/>
        </mxCell>

      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
</mxfile>
